---
import "../styles/global.css";

const basePath = import.meta.env.BASE_URL.replace(/\/?$/, "/");
---

<!doctype html>
<html lang="en">
  <head>
    <meta charset="UTF-8" />
    <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width" />
    <meta name="description" content="How Foxhole Forecast settles and scores timed model predictions." />
    <title>Scoring Method · Foxhole Forecast</title>
  </head>
  <body>
    <header class="method-masthead">
      <div class="masthead__eyebrow">EVENT OUTCOME PROTOCOL V4 · PUBLIC SCORING RULES</div>
      <div class="method-hero page-width">
        <div>
          <p class="kicker">Methodology</p>
          <h1>How forecasts<br /><span>earn credit</span></h1>
          <p class="sublede">A prediction must name the base, what happens, an exact ETA, and a confidence before the event. The official War API supplies the result.</p>
        </div>
        <a class="method-back" href={basePath}>← Return to live scores</a>
      </div>
    </header>

    <nav class="section-nav" aria-label="Scoring sections">
      <div class="page-width">
        <a href="#forecast">Forecast</a>
        <a href="#settlement">Settlement</a>
        <a href="#timing">Timing</a>
        <a href="#outcome">Outcome</a>
        <a href="#aggregation">Leaderboard</a>
        <a href="#censoring">Censoring</a>
        <a href="#rationale">Rationale</a>
      </div>
    </nav>

    <main class="scoring-page page-width">
      <section class="method-intro">
        <p class="kicker">The short version</p>
        <p>A correct event inside the API interval containing the predicted minute earns 1.00. Credit declines in 15-minute steps as the event moves away from the ETA, reaching 0.00 at three hours. Confusing a destruction with a completed capture earns 0.75 outcome credit; predicting a change when none occurs earns 0.00.</p>
        <aside class="method-callout"><strong>Headline score</strong><span>Average outcome credit × timing credit across every scoreable prediction.</span></aside>
      </section>

      <section id="forecast" class="scoring-section">
        <div class="scoring-section__heading"><p class="kicker">01</p><h2>What a model must forecast</h2></div>
        <div class="rule-grid">
          <article class="rule-card"><strong>8</strong><h3>Unique bases</h3><p>Every round contains exactly eight ranked predictions on eight different strategic bases.</p></article>
          <article class="rule-card"><strong>4 + 4</strong><h3>Two ETA ranges</h3><p>Four ETAs must be after the cutoff and within six hours. Four must be more than six and no more than 24 hours after the cutoff.</p></article>
          <article class="rule-card"><strong>2</strong><h3>Allowed events</h3><p><b>Captured</b> means ownership completes a change to the opposing faction. <b>Destroyed</b> means a faction-owned base becomes neutral—demolished and not yet rebuilt.</p></article>
          <article class="rule-card"><strong>1–5</strong><h3>Evidence references</h3><p>Each call cites cutoff-safe metrics and rates their relevance from 1–10. Evidence ratings are shown for auditability, but are not judged or added to the score.</p></article>
        </div>
        <p class="method-note">A neutral base can only be forecast as captured. Each prediction also includes an exact UTC minute and confidence from 0.00 to 1.00. Rank records the model’s priority, but does not change a prediction’s weight.</p>
      </section>

      <section id="settlement" class="scoring-section">
        <div class="scoring-section__heading"><p class="kicker">02</p><h2>How a prediction resolves</h2></div>
        <div class="rule-grid rule-grid--two">
          <article class="rule-card"><h3>Finite deadline</h3><p>Each prediction remains open through <code>ETA + 3 hours</code>. We look for the first physical ownership transition for that base after the round cutoff and before that deadline.</p></article>
          <article class="rule-card"><h3>Destruction before capture</h3><p>A faction-owned base normally becomes neutral before an enemy rebuild completes. If the model called a capture, we wait through the deadline for that follow-up. An enemy capture earns full outcome credit; destruction without a completed capture earns 0.75.</p></article>
        </div>
        <p class="method-note">If no transition occurs by the deadline and observation coverage is adequate, the prediction is a miss. The forecast is never left open forever and is never assigned an infinite ETA error.</p>
      </section>

      <section id="timing" class="scoring-section">
        <div class="scoring-section__heading"><p class="kicker">03</p><h2>Timing credit</h2></div>
        <p>The public API does not reveal the exact event second. Polling tells us that a change happened between the last observation and the first observation showing it. We therefore treat that span as the event’s observed interval.</p>
        <div class="formula-block">
          <span>distance</span>
          <strong>0 when the ETA is inside the observed interval; otherwise, minutes to its nearest edge</strong>
          <span>timing component</span>
          <strong>max(0, 1 − ceil(distance ÷ 15) ÷ 12)</strong>
        </div>
        <div class="credit-scale" aria-label="Timing credit examples">
          <span><b>Inside interval</b><strong>1.00</strong></span>
          <span><b>15 min</b><strong>0.92</strong></span>
          <span><b>30 min</b><strong>0.83</strong></span>
          <span><b>1 hour</b><strong>0.67</strong></span>
          <span><b>90 min</b><strong>0.50</strong></span>
          <span><b>2 hours</b><strong>0.33</strong></span>
          <span><b>2h 30m</b><strong>0.17</strong></span>
          <span><b>3+ hours</b><strong>0.00</strong></span>
        </div>
        <p class="method-caption">This is deliberately a 15-minute scale: asking for an exact minute makes the forecast concrete, but scoring does not pretend the API observed that minute exactly.</p>
      </section>

      <section id="outcome" class="scoring-section">
        <div class="scoring-section__heading"><p class="kicker">04</p><h2>Outcome and combined credit</h2></div>
        <div class="outcome-table" role="table" aria-label="Outcome credit table">
          <div class="outcome-table__row outcome-table__head" role="row"><span>Prediction vs. observation</span><span>Outcome credit</span></div>
          <div class="outcome-table__row" role="row"><span>Captured / Captured</span><strong class="score-value">1.00</strong></div>
          <div class="outcome-table__row" role="row"><span>Destroyed / Destroyed</span><strong class="score-value">1.00</strong></div>
          <div class="outcome-table__row" role="row"><span>Captured / Destroyed, or Destroyed / Captured</span><strong class="score-value">0.75</strong></div>
          <div class="outcome-table__row" role="row"><span>No qualifying transition by the deadline</span><strong class="score-value score-value--miss">0.00</strong></div>
        </div>
        <div class="formula-block formula-block--compact"><span>prediction credit</span><strong>outcome credit × timing component</strong></div>
        <div class="worked-example">
          <p class="kicker">Worked example</p>
          <h3>Correct capture, observed one hour late</h3>
          <p><code>1.00 outcome × 0.67 timing = 0.67 prediction credit</code></p>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section id="aggregation" class="scoring-section">
        <div class="scoring-section__heading"><p class="kicker">05</p><h2>Leaderboard and confidence</h2></div>
        <div class="rule-grid rule-grid--two">
          <article class="rule-card"><h3>Headline forecast score</h3><p>The leaderboard is the arithmetic mean of prediction credit across all scoreable predictions for a model, reported from 0.00 to 1.00. Every prediction has equal weight. Open and censored predictions are excluded.</p></article>
          <article class="rule-card"><h3>Confidence calibration</h3><p>We also retain a Brier-style squared error: <code>(confidence − prediction credit)²</code>. Lower is better. Because partial credit can be fractional rather than binary, this is an auxiliary calibration diagnostic—not the headline ranking score.</p></article>
        </div>
        <div class="worked-example">
          <p class="kicker">Continuing the example</p>
          <p>At 0.80 stated confidence and 0.67 prediction credit, the calibration error is <code>(0.80 − 0.6667)² ≈ 0.0178</code>.</p>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section id="censoring" class="scoring-section">
        <div class="scoring-section__heading"><p class="kicker">06</p><h2>When we decline to score</h2></div>
        <p>A prediction is <b>censored</b> when the observations cannot support a fair result. Censored is a completed settlement state, but it contributes neither credit nor a miss to the model average.</p>
        <ul class="rationale-list">
          <li><strong>Missing coverage.</strong> A gap greater than two expected polling intervals—currently 30 minutes—means we may have missed or badly localized a transition.</li>
          <li><strong>Ambiguous transition interval.</strong> For a coarse interval, we score only when every possible event time in that interval produces the same timing credit. Otherwise we censor it.</li>
          <li><strong>Certain coarse miss.</strong> A wide interval can still receive zero when even its closest possible event time is outside the credit window.</li>
        </ul>
      </section>

      <section id="rationale" class="scoring-section">
        <div class="scoring-section__heading"><p class="kicker">07</p><h2>Why score it this way?</h2></div>
        <ol class="rationale-list rationale-list--numbered">
          <li><strong>Make every claim falsifiable.</strong> A named base, event, exact ETA, and confidence is more informative than “something may happen in 24 hours.”</li>
          <li><strong>Force short- and longer-range judgment.</strong> The four-within-6h plus four-from-6–24h split prevents a model from filling a round only with safer, vague next-day bets.</li>
          <li><strong>Match scoring precision to observation precision.</strong> Fifteen-minute steps respect the collection cadence while retaining a clear reward for being closer.</li>
          <li><strong>Recognize the structure of Foxhole captures.</strong> Destroyed and captured are distinct outcomes, but both correctly anticipate loss of the current owner; the 0.75 rule captures that shared information.</li>
          <li><strong>Close every fair bet.</strong> The three-hour grace window permits near misses while ensuring non-events eventually become zero rather than remaining unresolved indefinitely.</li>
          <li><strong>Avoid subjective grading.</strong> Evidence and relevance ratings remain visible for human review, while numerical settlement depends only on prospective forecasts and official API observations.</li>
        </ol>
      </section>

      <p class="method-version">Current protocol: <code>event_outcome_v4</code>. Historical rounds made under older probability-horizon protocols may remain in the archive, but do not enter the current frontend leaderboard.</p>
    </main>

    <footer><div class="page-width"><strong>FOXHOLE FORECAST</strong><a href={basePath}>Live dashboard</a></div></footer>
  </body>
</html>
